    title: "Cartridge Filter vs Sand Filter: Pool Contractor's Complete Guide",
    metaTitle: "Cartridge Filter vs Sand Filter for Commercial Pools | Pool Supply Wholesalers",
    metaDescription: "Cartridge filter vs sand filter: which is right for your commercial pool project? Full comparison of filtration levels, water usage, maintenance, and cost. Includes sizing charts.",
    keywords: ["cartridge filter vs sand filter", "commercial pool filter", "pool filter comparison", "pool filtration guide", "pool filter sizing", "cartridge pool filter", "sand pool filter"],
    category: "filters",
    categoryLabel: "Filters",
    author: "Pool Supply Wholesalers",
    authorTitle: "Commercial Pool Equipment Specialists",
    date: "2026-08-20",
    dateModified: "2026-09-04",
    readTime: "7 min read",
    tags: ["cartridge filter", "sand filter", "pool filter comparison", "pool filtration guide", "commercial pool filter"],
    highlight: "Sizing Charts Included",
    excerpt: "Which filtration system is right for your project? We break down micron filtration levels, backwash requirements, water usage, maintenance intervals, and total cost of ownership for commercial pools.",
    image: "/images/blog/cartridge-filter-vs-sand-filter-pool-contractor-guide.jpg",
    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The filter vs filter debate comes up in almost every commercial pool spec meeting. Owners want the cheapest option; engineers want the lowest maintenance; health departments want the cleanest water. The right answer depends on your specific site conditions — and it's almost never one-size-fits-all." },
      { type: "h2", text: "How Each Filter Technology Works" },
      { type: "h3", text: "Cartridge Filters" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Cartridge filters use pleated polyester filter elements (cartridges) to physically strain particles from water as it passes through. The pleated design dramatically increases surface area — a 520 sq ft Pentair Clean & Clear Plus, for example, has more filter area than a comparably sized sand filter at a fraction of the water waste." },
      { type: "h3", text: "Sand Filters" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Sand filters push water through a bed of #20 silica sand (or newer zeolite/glass media). Particles get trapped in the sand bed as water passes downward. When pressure builds, you backwash — reversing flow to flush trapped debris to waste. The process takes 2–4 minutes but wastes 100–200+ gallons of backwash water per cycle." },
      { type: "h2", text: "Filtration Comparison: What Actually Gets Filtered" },
      { type: "table", headers: ["Filter Type", "Filtration Level", "What Gets Through", "Clarity Result"], rows: [
        ["Cartridge (standard)", "10–15 microns", "Fine oils, some bacteria", "Very clear — health code compliant"],
        ["Sand (silica)", "20–40 microns", "Fine particles, algae spores", "Clear but slightly hazy possible"],
        ["Sand (zeolite media)", "3–5 microns", "Most particles, ammonia", "Comparable to cartridge"],
        ["DE (diatomaceous earth)", "1–3 microns", "Near-total particle removal", "Crystal clear"],
      ]},
      { type: "paragraph", text: "For most commercial pools with strict health code requirements, cartridge filters at 10–15 microns provide excellent clarity without the water waste of backwashing. They're now the preferred choice for high-traffic commercial pools in California and Florida." },
      { type: "h2", text: "Water Usage: A Critical Commercial Consideration" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is where cartridge filters win decisively for water-conscious operations. Sand filters need backwashing every 2–4 weeks typically, using 150–250 gallons per backwash cycle. A busy commercial pool might need backwashing twice weekly — that's 300–500 gallons per week, or 15,000–26,000 gallons per year just in backwash water. At commercial water rates, that adds up fast." },
      { type: "callout", variant: "info", title: "Water Cost Example", text: "At $0.005 per gallon commercial water rate, backwashing a large sand filter twice weekly costs approximately $75–$130/year in water costs alone — plus chemicals to replace what's lost and the labor to backwash. Cartridge filters require no backwash water." },
      { type: "h2", text: "Maintenance Comparison" },
      { type: "table", headers: ["Task", "Cartridge Filter", "Sand Filter"], rows: [
        ["Routine cleaning", "Rinse cartridges every 6–12 weeks", "Backwash every 2–4 weeks"],
        ["Deep cleaning", "Soak cartridges in cleaner 2x/year", "Not required"],
        ["Media replacement", "Cartridges every 2–3 years ($150–400)", "Sand every 5–7 years ($80–200)"],
        ["Water usage", "Zero (no backwash)", "150–250 gal per backwash cycle"],
        ["Labor per maintenance", "20–45 minutes", "5–10 minutes per backwash"],
      ]},
      { type: "h2", text: "Filter Sizing Guide for Commercial Pools" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The golden rule for sizing: calculate at 1 sq ft of filter area per 10 GPM of flow rate (cartridge), or 1 sq ft of sand bed area per 15–20 GPM (sand). Under-sizing causes rapid pressure buildup and poor filtration. Commercial pools should always be sized at the larger end of the range." },
      { type: "table", headers: ["Pool Volume", "Flow Rate (GPM)", "Cartridge Filter Size", "Sand Filter Size"], rows: [
        ["25,000 gal", "30–40 GPM", "400–520 sq ft", "2.5–3 sq ft sand bed"],
        ["50,000 gal", "50–70 GPM", "520–700 sq ft", "3–4 sq ft sand bed"],
        ["100,000 gal", "80–120 GPM", "800–1,200 sq ft (multiple units)", "5–7 sq ft (large commercial unit)"],
      ]},
      { type: "h2", text: "Top Models by Category" },
      { type: "h3", text: "Best Cartridge Filters" },
      { type: "bullets", items: [
        "Pentair Clean & Clear Plus 520 — most-specified commercial cartridge filter, 520 sq ft",
        "Hayward C7030 Star-Clear Plus — 700 sq ft capacity for large commercial applications",
        "Jandy DEV600 — DE filter option for maximum clarity in high-inspection facilities",
      ]},
      { type: "h3", text: "Best Sand Filters" },
      { type: "bullets", items: [
        "Hayward Pro Series Sand Filter — most widely serviced commercial sand filter in the US",
        "Pentair Tagelus TA-100D — large commercial single-unit sand filter",
        "Jandy CS Series — strong performer for Jandy ecosystem builds",
      ]},
      { type: "h2", text: "Our Recommendation for Commercial Pools" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "For most commercial pool applications — hotel pools, apartment complexes, aquatic centers, and health clubs — we recommend cartridge filtration. The water clarity is better, water usage is zero, and modern cartridge elements last 2–3 years between replacements. Yes, the upfront cost is higher, but the total cost of ownership over 5 years is comparable or better when you factor in water savings and labor." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can I upgrade from a sand filter to a cartridge filter on an existing commercial pool?", a: "Yes, in most cases this is a straightforward equipment swap. You'll need to verify the plumbing connections match (most commercial filters use 2\" or 2.5\" PVC), and confirm the new filter's flow rate range is compatible with your existing pump. Pool Supply Wholesalers can help spec the right replacement." },
      { q: "How often do commercial pool cartridge filters need to be replaced?", a: "Commercial pool cartridge filter elements typically last 2–3 years with proper maintenance (rinsing every 6–8 weeks and chemical soaking 2x/year). In very high-bather-load facilities (water parks, competition pools), plan for replacement every 18–24 months." },
    ],
